#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b13d7123-12c3-5fba-865d-79d04d134405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366631} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb75fec-aad0-52b8-8f0b-256603947924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366632} {:answer/id #uuid "335c53ad-9b06-5ae9-9d82-ab45fdedd159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366633} {:answer/id #uuid "50865aa7-b849-5510-a201-43adab487ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366634} {:answer/id #uuid "30cf17d6-9da7-5a11-b299-70e72069e0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes  $3$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "335c53ad-9b06-5ae9-9d82-ab45fdedd159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366633} :answer-field/id #uuid "23070fd2-4a47-5c26-ae55-52058929b2df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c25a282-f09e-5080-ae4d-6a92631e6519" :question/math-academy-id "q-95397" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1})-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1)-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-2\\cdot 1-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4{y}_{2}+6 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ 2{z}_{1}^{2} & =4{z}_{2} \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & =2{z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ec62dfe-79c3-5df9-aac6-8a3a17aaaecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171358} {:answer/id #uuid "e6399f6c-b04f-5ecf-8053-726d3de3c39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171359} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf8347a-dfca-5952-96d3-427ecf3f2518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171360} {:answer/id #uuid "729cf01c-5ec6-5b07-a19b-839c49047a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171361} {:answer/id #uuid "7d71e306-7fc7-589b-914c-2f234e7856ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171362}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f31f2330-95df-50e5-a4cd-753dc047d887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171357}] :question/id #uuid "7dcde9f5-c70c-5225-9e3c-4744c661314a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95402" :question/problem "What type of a quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n$2x_{1}^{2} + 3x_{2}^{2} - 4x_{1} + 12x_{2} =-8$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259e4eb3-4b40-5254-9306-2cdbae4d082f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404866} {:answer/id #uuid "84eb9ae9-484d-5bb3-abcb-0e5d6c244735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404867} {:answer/id #uuid "a8704e70-86f6-5b66-8725-e7aa791842f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404868} {:answer/id #uuid "374654e9-d95a-5470-b619-3f7c557f6027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404869} {:answer/id #uuid "0f04bc86-c555-5e60-95dd-3ee129807d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "259e4eb3-4b40-5254-9306-2cdbae4d082f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404866} :answer-field/id #uuid "f725653a-5c15-5cd9-8b78-52c824803bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca23a2a3-705b-526c-8692-0652a1e980bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95998" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{17}{4}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{2}) & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-4+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents the single point $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050d3072-c838-5e08-b0bc-4d5171d8a819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366734} {:answer/id #uuid "e5183501-b9cd-5561-b6e3-c0b1b0bb5462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366735} {:answer/id #uuid "c04e7a5e-a95d-5547-bc90-7be6e5c15598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366736} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed40f62-9010-5b5c-bf46-6927e9dc6923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366737} {:answer/id #uuid "4d718142-0fc1-5c0e-bd69-6c41ab31ffc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d718142-0fc1-5c0e-bd69-6c41ab31ffc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366738} :answer-field/id #uuid "296453d6-3c71-5737-82c3-c5c36630c17c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb83c793-f479-5cb9-b03e-05eb6c2cfac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96075" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=\\frac{35}{3}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1})+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2})+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{36}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}.$"}]}
